Title:
COMegon: Putting Beacons to Bed with COM Internals

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post presents COMegon, a Windows “sleep/dispatch” technique that routes arbitrary API calls through COM’s cross-apartment RPC dispatch so the active call stack contains only signed system DLL frames (combase.dll/rpcrt4.dll/ntdll.dll).  
- It solves the common beacon-sleep detection problem where EDRs and hunting tools flag suspicious user-code return addresses, NtContinue-based context tricks, APC/timer callbacks, or anomalous stack frames during sleep/encryption windows.  
- The technique forges COM server-side metadata (CStdStubBuffer/CInterfaceStubHeader/MIDL_SERVER_INFO + NDR format strings) so NdrStubCall2 unmarshals parameters and calls attacker-chosen function pointers via COM’s normal message-pump path (ModalLoop/BlockFn/ThreadWndProc).  
- It uses an STA thread with a hidden window for message dispatch, a short-lived MTA helper to force cross-apartment posting (PostCall), and fibers to park the caller stack while a fresh fiber runs combase!ModalLoop.  
- It is useful for red team operators, implant developers, and malware researchers building stealthy sleep masks or “clean stack” API dispatch; it’s also relevant to defenders studying COM/RPC-based evasion and stack-based detections.  
- Notably, it is CET shadow-stack compatible because it relies on legitimate call/ret chains rather than fabricated CONTEXT/NtContinue transitions.

Technical Focus:
- COM apartments (STA/MTA), cross-apartment marshaling, hidden window message pumping
- COM/RPC internals: RPCOLEMESSAGE, IRpcChannelBuffer, PostCall/SendReceive
- Forged COM stub/proxy structures: CStdStubBuffer, CInterfaceStubHeader, MIDL_SERVER_INFO
- NDR (Oi2) procedure format strings and NdrStubCall2 dispatch
- Fibers (ConvertThreadToFiber/CreateFiber/SwitchToFiber) to isolate stacks
- Clean-stack sleep masking and CET (hardware shadow stack) constraints

Use Cases:
- Implementing stealthy beacon sleep with in-memory code encryption/decryption while avoiding user-code frames on live stacks
- Clean-stack execution of sensitive Win32/Nt APIs (allocation, protection changes, waits) via COM dispatch
- Researching COM/RPC attack surface and how metadata trust can be abused for control-flow redirection
- Evaluating/benchmarking stack-walk and “sleeping beacon” detection tools against COM-driven dispatch

Keywords:
COM, COM apartments, STA, MTA, combase.dll, rpcrt4.dll, ModalLoop, CCliModalLoop, ThreadWndProc, RPCOLEMESSAGE, IRpcChannelBuffer, PostCall, SendReceive, NdrStubCall2, MIDL_SERVER_INFO, NDR format string, CStdStubBuffer, fibers, SwitchToFiber, CET shadow stack, sleep mask, beacon evasion, MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx, CoMarshalInterThreadInterfaceInStream, CoRegisterClassObject